Unreleased
---
Bugfix
- The first action a top app bar was given wore the focus ring from the moment
  it was added, at eight per cent of ink over a part of the bar nothing had been
  done to. The ring is a mark that says where the keyboard is, and adding an
  action was putting the keyboard's place on the first icon before the keyboard
  had been anywhere - and the bar drew that place whether or not it had the
  keyboard at all. A mark is now drawn for the pointer wherever the pointer is,
  and for the keyboard only while the bar holds it; the keyboard's place is
  found the first time a key arrives, which is soon enough for a ring that is
  only true once a key has arrived.
- Destinations past the rail's room were unreachable by eye and reachable by
  accident at the same time: the column was laid out to its full extent and
  clipped by the window only, so the destinations below the bottom padding were
  invisible while their rectangles still caught a press aimed at the padding.
  The column now travels inside the room it was given - the wheel moves it a
  destination a notch, the keyboard keeps what it walks to in sight, putting a
  destination in force brings it into view, and what stands outside the room is
  clipped and beyond the pointer alike. A rail whose column fits does not
  travel, and the wheel it is not owed passes on.

0.1.1
---
Feature
- MaterialDialog, in the two shapes M3 gives a dialog. The difference between
  them is not size, it is what the thing is: a basic dialog is a question with
  an answer - a card at the 28 corner of the shape scale, over a scrim, holding
  a sentence and at most two answers - while a full-screen dialog is a place to
  do something in: full-bleed from the top of the page, its name in a bar with a
  cross that puts it away, and whatever the application wants in the middle.
  Qt's QDialog is the window underneath, because modality, Escape, exec() and a
  result code are the toolkit's; what is drawn here is the surface and the
  answers. The answers are text and not commands - a Material dialog does not
  fill an answer in, it names it in the accent and lets the press be the whole
  of it - which is why they are painted rather than widget buttons: a style
  sheet cannot draw a QPushButton without a border, and a text button is the
  whole of what an answer is here. They ask and do not decide: pressing one
  emits buttonClicked() and nothing else happens, because whether the question
  was answered, and which way that counts, is the application's. The cross of a
  full-screen dialog is the one exception, and it is not an answer - it is the
  dialog being put away, which is what a frameless window otherwise has no
  button for.
- MaterialTextField, the field that keeps its label. Qt has QLineEdit and a
  placeholder, and a placeholder is not this: it disappears the moment there is
  something to say, while a field's name has to go on standing when the field
  is filled - it is what the row means, not what it is waiting for. So the name
  is drawn: it stands where the text will be typed while the field is empty and
  has no keyboard, and floats, smaller, out of the way as soon as either is
  true. Two variants, the two answers Material gives to where a field ends:
  filled is an opening in the page tinted with the page's own ink, with an
  indicator line under it that thickens and takes the accent while the field
  has the keyboard; outlined is a stroke that opens a window where the floated
  label lands, because a stroke behind a twelve point label is a stroke through
  it. The text itself is a QLineEdit, so selection, the cursor, the clipboard
  and an input method behave the way the platform taught the user to expect,
  and the field reports what a single-line field has to report: textChanged
  whether the text was typed or set, editingFinished, and returnPressed.
- MaterialTopAppBar::actionAt(), which says what stands at a point in the bar
  and says nothing when that point is the bar itself. The leading icon and the
  trailing ones answer alike, because the question it answers is not which row
  a point is in but whether it belongs to an icon at all. A window that hands
  its whole app bar over as client area - the only way an icon the bar paints
  itself can be clicked - gives up the caption with it, and telling "the user
  pressed a button" from "the user pressed the caption" is what lets a caption
  gesture be given back without taking the buttons away again.

Bugfix
- MaterialDialog now answers the pointer over its answers and its cross. They
  are painted on the card rather than being widgets in it, and the card did not
  track the mouse, so a pointer that arrived over either of them was never
  spoken of: the wash under an answer - which is the only thing that says the
  dialog heard the pointer - never appeared, and an answer read as dead until
  the moment it was pressed. A press worked all along, because an ignored press
  travels up to the dialog; a move does not unless the widget under the pointer
  asked to be told where it is.

0.1.0
---
Feature
- The library opens. It is Material's answer to what a control is, drawn in
  Widgets: the corner scale, the type scale, the elevation levels and the
  duration-and-easing scale that the controls in here are drawn from, and the
  controls themselves as they land. It is one of three choices an application
  can make - ribbon, Fluent, Material - not a skin for any of them, and it
  shares nothing but mechanism with the Fluent one: the two answer the same
  questions differently, and a control that is both is a control that is
  neither.
- MaterialSwitch, the switch: a track that fills with the primary colour and a
  handle that crosses it, carrying a mark once it is on. Qt has a check box and
  no switch, and they are not the same answer - a switch says what it stands
  for is in force now, a check box says a choice was made and will be collected
  later. It is not Fluent's switch either: Fluent's is a small pill whose knob
  keeps its size, so its state has to be read by comparing the knob against
  the far end of the track, while this one can be read off the handle alone.
- MaterialSlider, the slider: a track, and a handle that rides over it - bigger
  than the track it covers, and lifting off it. The difference between the two
  families is what it does not draw: Fluent's slider says where its rail is
  divided, with a mark at every interval, and this one says nothing of the
  kind, because the handle's position is the answer and a second one would be
  a second answer to a question already answered. A press anywhere on the
  track moves the handle there, a drag follows the pointer at once, and the
  keyboard steps it - because a slider is a value in a range, so it brings its
  own accessible face and lets the value be read back and set back.
- MaterialBadge, the badge: a dot that says there is something, or a number
  that says how much. Qt has no badge, which is why a count ends up painted
  into a label, a button or a pixmap made at run time. It is not the Fluent
  one: a Fluent badge is the application pointing at something and wears the
  accent, while this one is the error colour, because what a badge says is that
  something needs attention - and past its maximum it stops counting and says
  so, because "127" is not more useful than "99+" and it is wider. A badge is
  a mark on something else, so it is neither a stop on the way through the
  keyboard nor something the pointer takes hold of.
- MaterialSegmentedButton, the segmented button: a row of choices, divided
  into equal parts, where the part in force is the one that is filled. Qt has
  radio buttons, and a radio group is the right answer for a form - which is
  not where a segmented button lives: it is a row inside a toolbar or a header,
  where a group of radio buttons arrives with a frame, spacing and a shape of
  its own. What says "one of these" here is the fill, not a marker travelling
  between the labels: the outline stays round the whole row. The row is one
  stop on the way through the keyboard, not one per segment, and the arrows
  move what is in force - because a segmented button is one of a fixed number
  of answers, and that is what a group of radio buttons is to an assistive
  tool, which is what each segment is announced as.
- MaterialChip, the chip: a small thing that stands for one choice, or does
  one thing. Qt has buttons for both, and a button is what a chip is made of -
  but a button in a row of buttons reads as a toolbar, while a row of chips
  reads as a set of things the user is carrying: a filter applied, a tag, a
  suggestion offered. The two kinds are not the same answer to the same
  question, which is why they are one class: an assist chip does something when
  it is pressed and holds nothing, while a filter chip holds a state, is filled
  while it is in force and carries a mark - because "which of these are on"
  has to be readable without pressing each one to find out. An assist chip is
  announced as a button and a filter one as a thing that is on or off.
- MaterialCard, the card: a surface that holds something, in one of three
  readings - elevated, which casts a shadow and is the page's own colour;
  filled, which is a surface one step from the page and casts nothing; and
  outlined, which is the page's own colour with a line round it. Qt has a group
  box for the same job, and it arrives with a frame and a title and reads as a
  form. It is not the Fluent card, and the difference is what each family
  thinks a card is: Fluent's says how far above the page it sits, so the lift is
  the message and a card is asked for an elevation; this one says which of
  three surfaces it is, so only one of the three casts a shadow at all. A
  Fluent card is lit from wherever the pointer is; this one answers the pointer
  by lifting one level - and takes no wash, because a tint on top of a lift
  would be two answers to one pointer. A card is a surface, not a button: what
  is inside it is what gets pressed.
- MaterialFAB, the floating action button: the one thing a screen is for,
  floated over it. Qt has buttons, and a button is what this is - but a button
  in a layout is one of the things on the screen, while this is *the* thing,
  and it says so by where it is: over the content, clear of it, lifted off it,
  and round. It sits at the highest level but the one that interrupts the
  screen, and it is the accent's own colour, because what it stands for is what
  the screen is for and that is what the accent is for. What it carries is an
  icon, and the icon is the application's - drawn as it was given, because
  recolouring it here would be guessing at what it meant - and what it says is
  not drawn at all, so it is what an assistive tool is told. The pointer lifts
  it rather than tinting it, which is one level more rather than another colour
  on top; and the wash it takes is the colour that reads on the accent, not the
  one that reads on the page.
- MaterialProgressRing, the progress ring: an arc that says how far along
  something is, over the track it has not reached, or an arc that only says
  something is happening. QProgressBar is a styled widget and its indeterminate
  mode is the style's to draw, so an application gets a block of Windows 95
  sweeping a track. The second mode is where this stops being the Fluent ring:
  that one is a fixed arc that turns, while this one grows and shrinks as it
  goes round - one movement rather than two, and a length that keeps changing is
  what says the wait has not settled. Neither is a transition, so both stop when
  the user asked for less motion, and both say how far along as a number an
  assistive tool can read back and set back.
- MaterialSnackbar, the snackbar: a sentence at the bottom of what is going on,
  that goes away on its own. Qt has status bars and message boxes, and neither
  is this: a status bar is a place that is always there, and a message box stops
  everything until it is answered. This arrives over the content, offers at most
  one thing to do about it, and leaves when it has been read - or when it is
  sent away, which the keyboard can do. It is the page's own colours turned
  over, which is what makes it a surface from somewhere else; that is not the
  Fluent info bar, which stays a surface of the page and says what it is with a
  severity colour and a place in the layout - a snackbar is not in the layout at
  all, and does not stay. A second thing to say does not make it arrive twice:
  it says the new thing where it is.
- MaterialSkeleton, the skeleton: the shape of what has not arrived yet. A
  screen that is waiting can show nothing and then jump, or it can show the
  shape of what is coming - a line where a line will be, a disc where a face
  will be - so that when it arrives it lands where the user was already
  looking. Material does not name this one, and it is drawn the way a surface
  here is: a tint of the page's own ink. What it does is breathe, because a
  shape that sits perfectly still is a shape the user has started to read - and
  breathing is the kind of movement the reduce-motion switch is about, so it
  stops there and holds the middle of its breath, which is still a shape. It is
  neither content nor a control: it takes neither the pointer nor a stop on the
  way through the keyboard, and it says nothing of its own.
- MaterialRipple, the ripple: the answer a surface gives when it is pressed. A
  press in most toolkits is a flat tint that switches on, which says "this was
  pressed" and nothing about where; this is a circle that starts where the
  pointer landed, spreads to the furthest corner and fades as it goes, so the
  surface answers from the place the user touched it. It is not the state layer
  and does not replace it - a hover gets the flat wash and a press gets the
  ripple on top of it - and it is not the Fluent reveal, which is the hover
  answer and stays where the pointer is, while a ripple is over before the
  pointer has moved. It is clipped to the corners of what it is on, and when
  the user asked for less motion there is none: a ripple is the movement, so
  there is nothing to draw without it. MaterialFAB wears it.
- materialtokens, the four scales Material arrives with, each spelled the way
  Material spells it and answered in the shared vocabulary: corners as radii
  (M3's seven collapsed to five, because two of them differ by four pixels and
  four pixels is not a difference a user can see), the type scale as sizes with
  a heavier cut, elevation as the shadow a level casts, and durations with the
  curve Material names alongside them - which is what makes a control that
  asks here honour the reduce-motion switch and the theme's motion scale
  without asking about either.
- MaterialNavigationRail, the column a Material application is navigated in
  when the room for it is narrow: eighty wide, holding three to seven
  destinations as an icon with its name under it, and a header above them that
  is given rather than drawn, because what goes there - a face, a mark - is the
  application's, not the rail's. It is the narrow half of what Fluent answers
  with one class: a Fluent pane folds to a strip of icons and opens again, and
  what it holds is one column of destinations beside the room they open into,
  while a rail is the whole width it has and never opens, and what it names is
  the middle of the three answers Material has - a bar for the narrowest, a
  rail for the narrow, a drawer for the rest. What says which destination is in
  force is a pill behind the icon, the accent at the strength Material gives an
  active indicator, and not a tint over the row: a row that is tinted reads as
  pressed in a language Material does not speak, and the pill is behind the
  icon rather than under the name because the icon is what is recognised
  first. A destination nobody can use is drawn thinned and cannot be put in
  force. The keyboard walks the column, and the column is announced as a list.
- MaterialTopAppBar, the top of a Material screen: one bar with three places in
  it - the leading icon, the name of the screen, and the trailing icons. Qt has
  no such bar: a QToolBar is a row of buttons whose look belongs to the style,
  a QMenuBar is a menu, and a title is a label the application has to place and
  keep placed. Which of the four shapes it takes is a question about the
  screen, not about taste: the two at fifty-six name the screen beside its
  icons, with the name in the middle in one of them, and the two taller ones
  give the name a row of its own - at twenty-two and at thirty-six - which is
  what a screen that is a place wants, and is why they are taller rather than
  bolder. What the bar says about being scrolled under is a change of surface,
  not a line and not a shadow: at rest it is the page, and once content has
  moved under it, it is the surface one step away - which is the whole of what
  Material calls on-scroll elevation, and why the scrolled state is handed to
  the bar rather than the bar watching a scroll area it was never given. An
  icon here is a QAction, and what a press does is the action's to say: the
  bar's only answer is which icon was landed on. The row is one stop on the way
  through the keyboard, not one per icon, and it is announced as a toolbar.

Bugfix
- Where the things in MaterialNavigationRail stand is now worked out when it is
  asked for rather than when the room changed, and the rail is fixed across.
  A widget that has never been shown is not told it was resized, so a rail asked
  where its destinations were answered from the geometry it had when it was last
  told - the width a top-level widget starts with, which put the column where it
  used to be and left a click aiming at nothing. Across the rail, the column is
  eighty wide wherever the rail was made wider than that and stands in the
  middle of the room it was given, and the rail is fixed at the token so that a
  layout cannot hand it the room that belongs to the screen - which is how a
  floating action button in its header came to sit off the middle of a rail a
  parent had stretched.
